00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.530
- I honestly-
- [Ali] Bribes again.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.070
- No, I honestly, I had to
let go a lot of the guilt.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.020
Like, so whenever I went
on tour with the kids,

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.030
well, first of all, I used
to travel with dogs too.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.280
- Oh my God.
- Before kids. Before kids.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.290
And then I was like, "Well,
I'm not gonna leave my dogs

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.300
at home, that's rude. They're
used to going with me, right?"

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.490
And then I was like, "This
is a horrible decision."

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.863
- Yeah.
- So the dogs went home.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.920
- How long do you last for with the dogs?

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.540
- Without the dogs?
- With the dogs.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:31.680
How long did you bring the dogs on?

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.963
- Maybe, maybe two weeks I lasted

00:02:33.963 --> 00:02:36.847
with children, dogs, and
work, like I was like,

00:02:36.847 --> 00:02:38.910
"This is insane."
- Mother of dragons.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:40.590
- Yeah, yeah. You feel-
- Many dependents.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.420
- That dies at the end.
I was that version.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.030
I was the one that was dying at the end.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.140
No, it's very hard.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.090
But I found that I had
to let go of the guilt

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:49.710
and I had help on the road.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:50.977
And I had to say like,

00:02:50.977 --> 00:02:52.710
"Guys, I can hang with y'all all night,

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.480
like after mommy's done",
but I don't go on stage,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:55.860
you don't go on stage till late at night.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:56.760
- Right.
- So it's like I had

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.770
to sleep in or my voice
would be like wrecked.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.300
- I know.
- And all those people pay,

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.290
you know, their hard-earned money

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:02.862
to like not watch you suck.
- Totally.

00:03:02.862 --> 00:03:04.950
- So, yeah. So, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.727
- So I had to tell them like,

00:03:06.727 --> 00:03:08.550
"This is the schedule for tour."

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.290
Like during life, I
mean, I dropped them off

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.730
at school this morning and I come to work.

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:14.559
It's a normal thing, but
when you're on the road,

00:03:14.559 --> 00:03:15.990
you're on the stage so late,

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.060
that it's like you have to
change your hours, I feel like.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.230
- Yes, it's true.
- Yeah.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.040
- And then it's like, "I'm
not reading to you anymore."

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.890
- [Kelly] Yeah, yeah.
- "That time is done.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:23.723
We're putting that on pause."

00:03:23.723 --> 00:03:24.937
- And when they give me crap, I'm like,

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:26.190
"Remember that video I sent you

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:27.570
with Ariana Grande talking to you?

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.487
Yeah, I don't wanna hear it."

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.478
I'm like, "No kid gets that."

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:33.780
(Ali laughing)
Remember that time?

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.330
I'm like, "You get some
cool perks, you know?

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.163
It's all right."

00:03:37.163 --> 00:03:38.280
It is hard though. It's very hard.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.250
And I do see why most men
leave children at home

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:42.750
'cause it's like, it's
not easy for them either.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.000
- Period. No, I'm just
kidding. I'm just kidding.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.080
- It's not easy for you to
do that work on the road

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:52.230
and also, like be a great present parent.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.820
That's really hard, I think.
- Yeah.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:57.480
I'm gonna do it, I think this
might be the last time I do it

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.640
because I hear that, you know, with kids-

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:00.960
- Well, then they start
school. It's harder.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.120
- Their lives get really important and big

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.160
and if they miss out on a birthday party

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:05.993
or something, they're like (groans).

00:04:05.993 --> 00:04:07.710
- [Kelly] Girl, you will hear about it.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.780
- Right. Now it's,
we're not at that point.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.720
So, this tour that I'm
going on this summer

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.100
will probably be like the last one

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.660
that I can take them and my mom on.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.610
- It's fun that it's summer
too. That's a perfect time.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:18.540
- [Ali] Yeah.
- For them to, yeah,

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:19.650
to go travel too.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:20.940
That's a different kind of education

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.170
I feel like my kids have gotten,

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:23.003
and your kids probably too.

00:04:23.003 --> 00:04:24.660
They get to travel around
to different cities and-

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:25.800
- It's true.
- Meet different people.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.210
Like that's not a normal education

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.860
for, well, how I grew up, so.
- Yeah.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.060
And it's so nice too

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.250
because like, you know how
in the city you live in,

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.350
you're like "I'm sick
of this science center.

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:35.226
I know where every single thing is."

00:04:35.226 --> 00:04:36.547
- I'm not going to the museum again."

00:04:36.547 --> 00:04:38.010
- "I'm not going to that museum again."

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.350
So it's so nice to go to
like a different park.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.430
- [Kelly] Yeah, a different aquarium.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.780
- Yes, a different aquarium.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.440
See like a different
albino alligator, you know?

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.270
- This one's so different
from the last one we saw.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.147
- Yeah, this one looks more like chicken

00:04:50.147 --> 00:04:52.710
than the other albino alligator.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.450
- Okay, do you have the,

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.300
do you feel like you have ability on stage

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.980
to talk about absolutely anything?

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.660
Do you feel like that kind of vulnerable?

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.420
I feel like you're that
person that is pretty open.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:04.253
- I'm pretty open.

00:05:04.253 --> 00:05:06.660
I can't, I'm like weirdly private too.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:08.430
Like I'm sure in writing your songs,

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.590
there's like, it seems like there's things

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:12.990
that you choose to be really open about

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:17.730
and there's a lot of
songs where I'm guessing

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.830
is like an abstraction of the truth.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:20.663
- Yeah.
- You know, and that's

00:05:20.663 --> 00:05:22.897
in the same way that like
a self-portrait is like,

00:05:22.897 --> 00:05:25.117
"Is your skin really that color?"

00:05:25.117 --> 00:05:25.950
"Do your eyes really look like that?"

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:29.310
And it's like, "No, this
is my like perception

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.560
of how I'm seeing myself right now."

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.480
And that's what you're
presenting to people.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.390
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- But that's also not forever.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:37.860
You know, that's not how
you see yourself forever.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.370
So that's the same thing
about like a song, right?

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:41.040
- Yeah, it's like different chapters too.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.060
- Different chapters.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.730
- So that's sort of how I see it.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:48.630
And I can't, I've tried to make
like political jokes before

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.010
and I just can't do it.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:53.310
I would love to be that
person, but I can't do it.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.770
- Are you too, like wholehearted
like opinionated about it?

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:56.615
Is that why? 'Cause I feel
like that's why I don't either.

00:05:56.615 --> 00:05:58.620
- I think I just start to get angry.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.570
- Me too. Like an inner rage

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:02.250
that I'm like, "Wow,
I'm such a bubbly person

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.150
but I'm getting very angry right now."

00:06:05.596 --> 00:06:07.580
So you're going back on the road soon.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.148
So when you're coming
up with new material,

00:06:09.148 --> 00:06:10.610
do you like rehearse it?

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:12.870
Do you like stand up in
like your room and do that?

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.900
- Yes. No, I don't. I write on stage.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.500
And I test out my material
at clubs and open mics.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.930
- So like fresh? Just yes.
- Like fresh, so it's very,

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.120
and so what I do a lot of the time

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.520
is when I, it's really
all about the writing.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.260
Like people think it's so
much about the performance,

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:30.060
but I mean, similar to music,

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.130
it's so much about word choice.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.790
So I'll go up on stage and
in front of an audience.

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.350
Like they will never know that I'm coming

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:39.000
'cause I'll just drop in at a club

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.710
and they'll be like so excited

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:42.720
and then I can see their faces fall

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:44.340
'cause I'm trying out new stuff.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.760
And I'll bomb and they'll literally,

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:49.860
I can see on their face,
like their inner thoughts

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:53.160
being like "She's lost it.
She's not funny anymore."

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:54.810
But it's like, "No, I'm
really trying to see

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:56.860
if the writing is good."
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:57.844 --> 00:06:59.460
- So I'm trying to take
the performance out of it.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:00.720
- That's so brave.
- So it'd be like

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.700
if you were like on-
- Testing out a song.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:03.630
- If you were testing out a song,

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:06.202
instead of singing, you
just spoke the lyrics.

00:07:06.202 --> 00:07:08.490
(both laughing)

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:09.360
In a monotone tone.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:10.337
- You get to guess how the melody is.

00:07:10.337 --> 00:07:12.437
- Since you've been gone. Yeah.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:15.360
- This is so cool. I love Michelle Obama.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:16.680
And she recently posted about you,

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.440
saying that you are one
of her favorite comedians.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.480
So how did that feel? That's pretty rad.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:22.380
Just to have fans, in general,

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:24.060
but somebody like her
to notice, that's cool.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:25.080
- It felt amazing.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:28.980
She had actually reached
out to me a couple years ago

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.470
and sent me a letter,
like a handwritten letter

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.060
and it's framed in my house,

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:36.960
where she wrote me about
how touched she was

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:39.330
that I talk about women's bodies

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:40.560
and what happens to our bodies

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.360
and make us laugh at the same time.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:44.370
And she was like, "I think
that's really important."

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.740
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- And so, yeah, of course.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:48.380
I mean, it's her.
- That's cool. Yeah.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.340
- So it means the world to me.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.380
- Yeah.
- And I have not

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:53.213
yet to meet her.

00:07:53.213 --> 00:07:55.350
She was, before COVID, she was set to come

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.450
to one of my shows in DC

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:59.880
and I think she bought like 25 seats.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:04.713
I'm sure half of them were for
Secret Service but whatever.

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:06.690
They paid for the tickets,

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:08.760
so I appreciate it.
- [Kelly] Regardless.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:13.440
- Yeah, and so I'm really, I
hope she comes this summer-

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:14.370
- Oh my gosh.
- To my show in DC.

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.440
- You're so funny, I cannot.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:18.510
And especially if she's
watching this new show of yours,

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:22.230
we're gonna get to "Beef"
because "Beef" is so relatable

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:23.227
and I know you're probably like,

00:08:23.227 --> 00:08:24.802
"Wait, it's relatable.
What's wrong with you?"

00:08:24.802 --> 00:08:26.100
(Ali laughing)

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:27.510
And that's what you're thinking now too

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:28.710
if you've watched it.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:31.290
But let's talk about it 'cause
it's your new Netflix series.

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.210
It's called "Beef".

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:35.910
Tell everybody what it's about,
like kind of in a synopsis.

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:38.970
